Fix voice rooms failing on Railway: state writes must never break lookups

The join-to-create voice hubs did nothing on Railway. Root cause was not the
voice code but the state store.

findChannel and findCategory write to state.json as a side effect of reading:
that is how the server heals itself after a manual rename. Railway mounts its
volume root-owned while the container runs as the unprivileged node user, so
every one of those writes raised EACCES, and the exception propagated out of
what the caller believed was a read. Channel resolution therefore threw inside
onVoiceStateUpdate, the ready handler and the Discord log transport.

Two fixes:

- The state store now updates its in-memory cache first and unconditionally,
  and swallows a failed write after reporting it once. Losing persistence is a
  mild degradation; losing every channel lookup is not. `isPersistent` is
  surfaced in /server-status so the degraded mode is visible rather than
  silent.

- A Docker entrypoint takes ownership of the data directory as root, then
  drops to the node user via su-exec. .gitattributes pins LF endings so the
  script does not arrive in the container with a CRLF shebang.

Also drops startCommand from railway.json so the image's own entrypoint runs.

